the centre of a group is defined as $Z(G) = {g in G : g x = x g forall x in G}$

#note[$Z(G) = C_G (G)$ and is a subgroup of $G$] 

])

#definition[Centraliser of an element][
Let $G $ be a group and $a in G$ fixed

The centraliser of $a $  in $G $ is the set $C(a)={g in G: g a g^(-1) = a} = {g in G : g a = a g}$ 

]

#prob[Let $G $ be a group. Show that $Z(G) = sect_(a in G) C(a)$ ][show set equality

$subset.eq$ \
Let $g in Z(G)$ then $g a = a g forall a in G$. \
Fix any $a in G$ then $g in {g in G:g a = a g} = C(a)$ \
Then $g in sect_(a in G) C(a)$ 

$supset.eq$ \
Let $g in sect_(a in G) C(a)$ then for any $a in G$ we have $g in C(a)$  i.e. that $g a = a g forall a$ \

This implies that $g in {g in G:g a = a g forall a in G} = Z(G)$

$qed$ 

]


#prob[Let $G $ be an abelian group and $H = {x in G: abs(x) "is odd"}$Prove that $H $ is a subgroup of $G$  ][

Firstly, $1 in H$ since $|1| = 1$ therefore $H eq.not emptyset$ \

Next, note that $forall x in H, |x^(-1)| = |x| arrow.double abs(x^(-1)) "is odd" arrow.double x^(-1) in H$

Next, let $a,b in H$ and set the orders $m:=abs(a), n:=abs(b), r :=lcm(m,n)$ $arrow.double r = m k, r = n l$ for some integers $k,l$

Then $(a b)^r = a^r b^r = a^(m k) b^(n l) = (a^m)^k (a^n)^l = 1$ since $G$ is abelian, therefore the order of $a b$ is $r = lcm(m,n)$

Since $m,n$ are odd then $lcm(m,n)$ is $"odd"^1$ and therefore $a b$ has odd order i.e. $a b in H$

Then since $H$ is a subset of $G$, finite, closed, and each element of $H$ has an inverse in $H$ then the claim follows. $qed$     

$1$: since $a b = gcd(a,b) dot lcm(a,b)$ (for positive integers $a,b$ )

